Problem Statement1
------------------
-> There is an array that have roll no and assign course to that roll number.
-> The roll number will repeat again in the array with different course name.
-> Now you have array with same roll number and different roll number with assign course.
-> Find different pairs of roll numbers eg: AB, BC, CD and find common courses among them and if they don't have any common course print empty array.

We write the logic approach step by step
----------------------------------------
-> First we get the input as String[][] something like below
	String[][] input = {
		{"A", "Math"},
		{"A", "Java"}
		{"A", "Sql"}
		{"B", "Java"}
		{"B", "Python"}
		{"C", "Python"}
		{"C", "AWS"}
	}
-> We group them by roll number and course.
-> get Unique roll numbers
-> generate unique pairs
-> intersect their course sets
-> store pair with common courses.

Analyzing time complexity
-------------------------
N - number of input rows
R - number of unique roll numbers
C - average number of course per roll
1. Grouping
-----------
-We visit every input row once
-So, Hashmap lookup and HashSet.add() are average O(1)
-> Time would be O(n)
2.Generating unique pairs
-------------------------
-> we have outer loop and inner loop
-> If there are R students then number of unique pairs are
	-> R * (R-1)/2
	-> Example with 4 students
	-> 	a-b,
		a-c,
		a-d,
		b-c,
		b-d,
		c-d
	-> That means 4 * (4-1) /2 - 6
	-> O(R2)
3.Intersection
--------------
-For each pair we do
	- If CourseA has C courses
	- loop through coursesA - O(c)
	- Hashset.contains() - O(1) average
	- HashSet.add() - O(1) average
	- therefore pair costs O(C)
4.Combining pair generation and intersection
---------------------------------------------
-> O(R2) pairs
-> O(C)
-> O(R2) * O(C)
-> O(R2 * C)
-> So total time complexity would be O(N) + O(R2 * C)

Common Interview follow-ups
---------------------------
1. Why did we use set instead of list?
-> Set automatically removes duplicates and provides faster lookups
-> Since we only care about unique courses and intersection, set is more suitable data structure.
2. What if no common courses found
-> Then the intersection set will be empty and i'll print empty array as required.
3.Can this solution be extended to all roll combinations instead of adjacent pairs.
-> Yes, instead of adjacent pairs, i can generate all combinations using nested loops and apply same intersection logic.
